All three findings fixed, each proven failing-first by mutation, all four gates green.

## What was wrong

All three findings were one defect seen from two sides: the engines asked the member predicate about the **member** and never about the **receiver**.

**1. BLOCKER** — `src/interpreter.rs` / `src/bytecode/vm.rs` fell through to `call(&qualified)` for any receiver, so a bound `<receiver>_<member>` was enough to make the call. `random.seed(1)`, `.number`, `.choice`, `.shuffle` all answered through `random_seed` etc., in both engines. There is no `random` module.

**2. MAJOR** — the round-6 check asked the stdlib table *before* the program's own, so `module text … end` publishing `greet` was refused. All 11 stdlib names refused the same way, though `declare_module` accepts them.

**3. MAJOR** — `SPEC.md:1056` claimed `length` answers a record; `runtime.rs`, `stdlib.rs`, `tests/test_text.rb:132` and `corpus/stdlib-0005` all say it is refused.

## The fix

`stdlib::dotted_call_reaches` (`src/stdlib.rs`) is now the whole rule in one place — the receiver must name a module, a program-declared module owns its own name, a stdlib module answers only for a pair `MODULE_MEMBERS` holds — and both engines *call* it rather than each writing half. `is_module_name` (the predicate that conflated the two) is gone.

## Gates added, none weakened

| New test | Claim | Universe comes from |
|---|---|---|
| `edge_a_registered_builtin_is_not_a_module_namespace` | 14 non-module `<prefix>_<member>` pairs refused in both engines; the 4 live `random` ones named; flat spellings behind each prefix asserted still answering | the **registry**, not the table the gate audits |
| `edge_a_module_the_program_declares_owns_its_own_name` | all 11 stdlib names as a program `module … end`; its member answers, its missing member still refused by dotted name | `stdlib::MODULES` |
| `edge_length_answers_a_text_or_a_list_and_refuses_a_record` | 3 spellings × text/list/record, **and** § text read out of SPEC.md | the document |

Mutations that each gate catches (run, then undone): the pre-fix fall-through → `random.number answered`; stdlib-table-first → `member table decided a receiver it says nothing about`; the drifted sentence restored → `SPEC.md § text does not say that a record is refused`.

